/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/linux-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.register({"id":"linux-plus","vendor":"CompTIA","name":"CompTIA Linux+","short":"Linux+","exam":"XK0-006","blurb":"Vendor-neutral Linux administration certification covering system management, services and users, security, automation and scripting, and troubleshooting, for junior sysadmins and cloud/DevOps engineers.","status":"check","statusNote":"comptia.org could not be opened from this environment on Sept 24, 2026 (network block), so the official page was not read directly. CompTIA's own search listings for the Linux+ V8 page show exam XK0-006 (launched July 15, 2025), up to 90 questions, 90 minutes and a 720 passing score, with XK0-005 retired January 13, 2026 in English. The domain weights (23/20/18/17/22) come from secondary sources and still need to be checked against the official XK0-006 objectives PDF, as do the objective numbers used in the question references.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-01-13","until":"2026-12-31","text":"The previous exam, XK0-005 (Linux+ V7), retired on January 13, 2026 in English (April 14, 2026 in Japanese, Portuguese and Spanish). Study for XK0-006 only."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Up to 90 (multiple choice and performance-based)","minutes":90,"pass":"720 on a 100–900 scale"},"examSim":{"questions":90,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CompTIA Linux+ (V8, XK0-006) certification page","url":"https://www.comptia.org/en-us/certifications/linux/"},{"label":"CompTIA blog: The new CompTIA Linux+, your questions answered","url":"https://www.comptia.org/en-us/blog/the-new-comptia-linux-your-questions-answered/"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"System management","w":23,"topics":["Boot process: UEFI/BIOS, GRUB2, kernel, initramfs (dracut, mkinitramfs), systemd targets","Filesystem Hierarchy Standard: /etc, /var, /usr, /opt, /home, /boot, /proc, /sys, /dev","Kernel modules and parameters: lsmod, modprobe, modinfo, /etc/modprobe.d, sysctl","Files and directories: ls, find, cp, mv, hard vs symbolic links, file, stat","Storage: partitions (fdisk, gdisk, parted), lsblk, blkid, UUIDs, /etc/fstab options (nofail, noexec)","LVM (pvcreate, vgextend, lvextend -r) and software RAID with mdadm","Filesystems: ext4, XFS, Btrfs; mkfs, mount, resize2fs, xfs_growfs; df and du","Network configuration: ip, nmcli, netplan, hostnamectl, /etc/hosts, /etc/resolv.conf, nsswitch.conf","Shell operations: redirection, pipes, environment variables, grep, sed, awk, cut, sort, uniq, tr","Backup and restore: tar, gzip/xz/bzip2, rsync, dd, cpio","Virtualization: KVM/QEMU, libvirt and virsh, virt-install, qcow2 vs raw images"],"notes":["Domain 1.0 objectives (confirm numbering in the XK0-006 objectives PDF)"],"labs":["In a free VirtualBox or KVM VM (Rocky Linux or Ubuntu Server), add a second virtual disk, then build PV, VG and LV, format it XFS, mount it by UUID in /etc/fstab and grow it with lvextend -r.","Break and fix the boot: add a bad /etc/fstab line, reboot into emergency mode, remount / read-write and repair it; then rebuild the initramfs with dracut -f or update-initramfs -u.","Use nmcli to give the VM a static IP, set its hostname with hostnamectl, and practice tar, rsync --delete and dd backups to a second disk."]},{"id":2,"name":"Services and user management","w":20,"topics":["Local accounts and groups: useradd, usermod -aG, userdel, groupadd, passwd, chage","Account files: /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group, /etc/skel, /etc/login.defs","systemd units: systemctl start/stop/enable/mask, status output, drop-in overrides with systemctl edit","Scheduling: cron and crontab syntax, at, systemd timers (OnCalendar)","Processes and jobs: ps, top, kill signals, nice/renice, bg/fg/jobs, nohup","Package management: dnf/rpm, apt/dpkg, repositories, provides and file ownership queries","Source and language packages: make, pip, sandboxed packages (Flatpak, Snap)","Containers: podman/docker run, images, port publishing, volumes, logs, inspect","Container orchestration concepts: Kubernetes pods, deployments, services","Logging: journalctl filters, rsyslog, logrotate"],"notes":["Domain 2.0 objectives (confirm numbering in the XK0-006 objectives PDF)"],"labs":["Create three users and a shared group on a free Linux VM, set password aging with chage, lock one account and inspect /etc/shadow to see the change.","Write a small systemd service and a .timer unit that runs a script daily, add an override with systemctl edit, and read its logs with journalctl -u.","Install podman, run nginx with -p 8080:80 and a named volume, recreate the container and confirm the data survives."]},{"id":3,"name":"Security","w":18,"topics":["Permissions: chmod symbolic and octal, chown, umask","Special permissions: SUID, SGID, sticky bit; ACLs with setfacl and getfacl","SELinux: modes, contexts, restorecon, semanage, booleans, ausearch; AppArmor profiles and modes","Firewalls: firewalld zones and --permanent, ufw, nftables/iptables basics","SSH hardening: key-based auth, ssh-copy-id, sshd_config (PermitRootLogin, PasswordAuthentication)","Privilege escalation: sudo, visudo, /etc/sudoers.d, su, polkit","Authentication: PAM modules (pam_faillock, pam_pwquality), LDAP/SSSD, Kerberos, MFA","Cryptography: hashing (sha256sum), GPG signatures, TLS certificates, LUKS disk encryption","OS hardening: disabling unused services, secure boot, patching, file integrity (AIDE)","Compliance and auditing: auditd, log review, vulnerability scanning, CIS benchmarks"],"notes":["Domain 3.0 objectives (confirm numbering in the XK0-006 objectives PDF)"],"labs":["On a Rocky Linux VM with SELinux enforcing, move a file from your home directory into /var/www/html, watch Apache return 403, find the AVC with ausearch -m avc and fix it with restorecon.","Harden SSH: create an Ed25519 key, install it with ssh-copy-id, set PermitRootLogin no and PasswordAuthentication no, and test before closing your session.","Configure firewalld (or ufw on Ubuntu) to allow only SSH and HTTPS, make the rules permanent, reboot and verify; then encrypt a spare virtual disk with cryptsetup luksFormat."]},{"id":4,"name":"Automation, orchestration, and scripting","w":17,"topics":["Bash scripting: shebang, variables, parameter expansion, quoting, exit codes and $?","Bash control flow: if/test, case, for and while loops, functions, arrays","Safer scripts: set -euo pipefail, trap, input validation, shellcheck","Python basics for admins: data types, sets and dicts, venv, pip, running scripts","Git: clone, branch/switch, add, commit, merge, rebase, revert, pull requests","Ansible: inventory, ad hoc commands, playbooks, idempotence, roles, ansible-vault","Puppet and other agent-based tools; OpenTofu/Terraform plan and apply","CI/CD pipelines and GitOps concepts","Responsible use of AI tools for scripting: review, testing, keeping secrets out of prompts"],"notes":["Domain 4.0 objectives (confirm numbering in the XK0-006 objectives PDF)"],"labs":["Write a Bash script that checks disk use with df, logs a warning above 80% and exits non-zero; run it through shellcheck and schedule it with a systemd timer.","Install Ansible on one VM, manage two others over SSH, write an idempotent playbook that installs and starts nginx, and run it twice to see changed vs ok.","Put your scripts in a Git repository, work on a feature branch, merge it, then practice git revert on a bad commit."]},{"id":5,"name":"Troubleshooting","w":22,"topics":["Storage issues: full disks, inode exhaustion (df -i), deleted-but-open files (lsof +L1), fsck/xfs_repair","Performance: load average, top/htop, vmstat, iostat, sar, free, OOM killer","Networking: ping, ip route, ss, dig/resolvectl, traceroute/tracepath/mtr, tcpdump, nmap","Boot problems: GRUB menu, previous kernels, emergency and rescue targets, fstab errors","Service failures: systemctl status exit codes, journalctl, dependencies, port conflicts","Security issues: SELinux denials, file permissions, SSH key permissions, locked accounts","Hardware: dmesg, lspci, lsusb, smartctl, failing disks and RAID degradation","Time sync: chrony, timedatectl, clock skew effects on TLS and Kerberos","Package problems: broken dependencies, repository errors, held packages","Container problems: logs, port conflicts, image pulls, storage"],"notes":["Domain 5.0 objectives (confirm numbering in the XK0-006 objectives PDF)"],"labs":["Fill a small test filesystem with 100,000 empty files to trigger inode exhaustion, then compare df -h and df -i and clean up.","Delete a large log that a process still has open, find it with lsof +L1, and free the space by restarting the process.","Remove the default route on a VM, diagnose with ip route, ping and traceroute, fix it persistently with nmcli, then break DNS and diagnose it with dig."]}],"study":{"1":[["Walk through the Linux boot process on a UEFI system.","Firmware (UEFI) runs the boot loader (shim/GRUB2) from the EFI system partition. GRUB2 loads the kernel and initramfs. The initramfs loads the drivers needed to mount the real root filesystem, then hands off to systemd (PID 1), which starts units until it reaches the default target."],["How do you add a new disk to an existing logical volume and grow the filesystem?","pvcreate /dev/sdX, vgextend <vg> /dev/sdX, then lvextend -r -L +SIZE (or -l +100%FREE) <lv>. -r runs resize2fs or xfs_growfs for you. XFS can grow but never shrink."],["What is the difference between a hard link and a symbolic link?","A hard link is another name for the same inode (same data, link count goes up) and cannot cross filesystems or point to directories. A symbolic link is a separate small file holding a path; it can cross filesystems and breaks if the target is removed."],["Why mount by UUID in /etc/fstab, and what do nofail and noexec do?","Device names like /dev/sdb can change between boots, but UUIDs (from blkid) stay the same. nofail lets boot continue if the device is missing; noexec blocks running programs from that filesystem."],["Which changes made with ip are lost at reboot, and how do you make them persistent?","Addresses and routes added with ip addr/ip route live only in the running kernel. Make them persistent with nmcli connection modify (NetworkManager), netplan YAML on Ubuntu, or the distribution's network files."],["Compare tar, rsync and dd for backups.","tar bundles files (with -z/-J for compression) into an archive. rsync copies or mirrors efficiently, sending only differences, and --delete makes an exact mirror. dd copies raw blocks, which is good for disk images but copies empty space too and is dangerous if if= and of= are swapped."]],"2":[["What is the difference between usermod -G and usermod -aG?","-G sets the full list of supplementary groups and removes any not listed. -aG appends the new group and keeps existing memberships."],["Explain enable, start, mask and the difference between enabled and active.","start runs a unit now; enable makes it start at boot; enable --now does both. mask links the unit to /dev/null so it cannot start at all. Enabled describes boot behavior and active describes the current state."],["Why use systemctl edit instead of editing files in /usr/lib/systemd/system?","Package updates overwrite files under /usr/lib. systemctl edit creates a drop-in in /etc/systemd/system/<unit>.d/ that survives updates and overrides only the settings you change."],["Read the crontab line '0 */6 * * 1-5 /opt/sync.sh'.","Minute 0 of every 6th hour (00:00, 06:00, 12:00, 18:00) Monday through Friday. Fields: minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week."],["When would you send SIGTERM, SIGKILL and SIGHUP?","SIGTERM (15) asks a process to exit cleanly and is the default. SIGKILL (9) forces an exit that cannot be caught, used when SIGTERM fails. SIGHUP (1) often tells daemons to reload their configuration."],["How do you keep container data when the container is replaced?","Store it in a named volume (-v name:/path) or a bind mount (-v /host/path:/path). The container's writable layer is deleted with the container."]],"3":[["What permissions does umask 027 give new files and directories?","Files start at 666 and become 640 (rw-r-----). Directories start at 777 and become 750 (rwxr-x---)."],["Explain SUID, SGID and the sticky bit with an example of each.","SUID (4xxx) runs a program as its owner, e.g. /usr/bin/passwd. SGID (2xxx) on a directory makes new files inherit the directory's group, which is useful for team shares. Sticky (1xxx) on a directory lets only a file's owner delete it, as on /tmp."],["A web server gets permission denied under SELinux. How do you troubleshoot it?","Check the mode with getenforce, find denials with ausearch -m avc -ts recent, compare file contexts with ls -Z, fix labels with restorecon (or semanage fcontext + restorecon), and enable a boolean with setsebool -P if needed. Do not just disable SELinux."],["List four SSH hardening steps.","Use key-based authentication (ssh-copy-id); set PasswordAuthentication no; set PermitRootLogin no; limit users with AllowUsers or AllowGroups; optionally change the port or rate-limit with fail2ban. Test in a second session before logging out."],["Why use visudo and /etc/sudoers.d?","visudo locks the file and checks syntax before saving, so a mistake cannot lock everyone out of sudo. Drop-in files keep rules modular and easy to remove."],["What is the difference between a runtime and a permanent firewalld rule?","Runtime rules take effect immediately but are lost on reload or reboot. --permanent rules are written to configuration and apply after firewall-cmd --reload. Use --runtime-to-permanent to save working runtime rules."]],"4":[["What does set -euo pipefail do?","-e exits on an unhandled command failure, -u treats unset variables as errors, and -o pipefail makes a pipeline fail if any command in it fails, not just the last one."],["Explain ${VAR:-default}, ${VAR:=default} and ${#VAR}.",":- uses the default when VAR is unset or empty without changing VAR. := uses and assigns the default. ${#VAR} gives the string's length."],["What makes an Ansible playbook idempotent, and why does it matter?","Modules describe the desired state (package present, service started) and change things only when they differ, so reruns are safe and report ok. Raw shell or command tasks are not idempotent unless you add creates/removes or changed_when."],["When do you use git revert instead of git reset?","Use revert on shared branches because it adds a new commit that undoes the change without rewriting history. reset moves the branch pointer and suits only local, unpushed work."],["How does OpenTofu/Terraform differ from Ansible?","OpenTofu is declarative infrastructure as code that tracks state and provisions resources (VMs, networks, cloud services) through plan and apply. Ansible mainly configures existing systems over SSH, though it can also provision."],["How should an admin use AI-generated scripts safely?","Read and understand every line, test on a non-production VM, run shellcheck or linters, never paste secrets or sensitive data into prompts, and put the script through normal code review and version control."]],"5":[["A disk shows free space but writes fail with 'No space left on device'. What do you check?","Run df -i for inode exhaustion (lots of tiny files), check for deleted-but-open files with lsof +L1, check quotas, and check whether reserved blocks apply to non-root users."],["How do you read load average?","It is the 1-, 5- and 15-minute average of tasks that are running, runnable or in uninterruptible wait. Compare it with the CPU count (nproc). Load well above the CPU count with low CPU use often points to I/O wait, which you check with iostat -x or vmstat."],["Give a layered method for 'server can't reach the internet'.","Check the link and address (ip addr), route to the gateway (ip route, ping the gateway), external IP (ping 8.8.8.8), DNS (dig, /etc/resolv.conf), then the path (traceroute) and firewall rules (nft list ruleset, firewall-cmd --list-all)."],["A service won't start. What steps do you follow?","systemctl status for the exit code (such as 203/EXEC), journalctl -u <unit> -b for details, check the config file syntax, ports in use (ss -tulpn), permissions and SELinux denials, and dependencies (systemctl list-dependencies)."],["How do you recover from an fstab mistake that stops boot?","In emergency mode, enter the root password, run mount -o remount,rw /, fix /etc/fstab (compare UUIDs with blkid), run systemctl daemon-reload, test with mount -a, then reboot."],["How do you confirm the kernel killed a process for lack of memory?","Search journalctl -k or dmesg for 'Out of memory' or 'Killed process'. Then review memory use with free -h, top/ps sorted by RSS, and the service's limits (MemoryMax in the unit or container limits)."]]},"qCount":91,"hasLessons":true});
